in from the second bone that may pull it a bit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:31.840 --> 00:34:38.000
towards a different direction. Blender provides&nbsp;
the artist with powerful tools to set the weights&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:38.000 --> 00:34:44.080
of the different bones. Most artists will probably&nbsp;
start with an automatic assignment of weights in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:44.080 --> 00:34:51.280
this method, Blender calculates the weights based&nbsp;
on the distance between the vertex and each bone.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:51.280 --> 00:34:57.440
The next step will be to review the result and&nbsp;
start fixing and adjusting and tuning the model&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:57.440 --> 00:35:03.120
using weight painting. Weight painting is a&nbsp;
feature in Blender where you increase or decrease&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:03.120 --> 00:35:10.640
the weights of vertices for the selected bone by&nbsp;
going over them with a special brush. Usually,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:10.640 --> 00:35:17.120
the artist will develop the skin first and then&nbsp;
structure the skeleton inside it. It makes sense&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:17.120 --> 00:35:22.000
because the bones must match the dimension of&nbsp;
the body part that will actually get rendered.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:22.640 --> 00:35:28.560
At this stage before the animation processes&nbsp;
started. The posture of the skin is called&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:28.560 --> 00:35:36.080
Bind Pose. This is very important because all the&nbsp;
underlying transformations and math will reference&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:36.080 --> 00:35:41.280
the Bind Pose as a starting position. There&nbsp;
are no restrictions in terms of how the model&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:41.280 --> 00:35:47.440
should look like in Bind Pose. But the common&nbsp;
practice is to keep the model in this kind of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:47.440 --> 00:35:52.960
relaxed posture without too many bendings&nbsp;
in the area of the joints. If you search for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:52.960 --> 00:35:59.040
skillful animation Bind Pose in Google Images,&nbsp;
you will find many examples of this posture&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:59.600 --> 00:36:05.200
for the human or semi human models, with the&nbsp;
arms kind of stretched to the sides and the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:05.200 --> 00:36:11.680
legs are straight and relaxed. When you render&nbsp;
the model without applying any animation on it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:11.680 --> 00:36:17.120
you should get it in Bind Pose. Once rigging&nbsp;
and skinning have been completed, the model is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:17.120 --> 00:36:23.920
ready for animation. Naturally, the same set of&nbsp;
bones can be used for multiple sets of animation.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:24.560 --> 00:36:30.560
Think about all the possibilities the human body&nbsp;
provides in terms of movement and flexibility. All&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:30.560 --> 00:36:38.160
this is achieved with just 206 bones. Even with&nbsp;
a small subset of these bones in a human model,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:38.160 --> 00:36:44.800
you can still implement many animations. Therefore&nbsp;
each animation set will simulate some kind of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:44.800 --> 00:36:52.640
activities such as walking, running, fighting,&nbsp;
etc. An animation set is composed of a series of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:52.640 --> 00:36:58.960
transformations that are applied on the skeleton&nbsp;
as the artist animates it. These transformations&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:58.960 --> 00:37:05.360
may include as usual scaling, rotation and&nbsp;
translation. In the case of humans in many&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:05.360 --> 00:37:11.600
other living creatures, we will only encounter&nbsp;
rotation and translation. But the technique itself&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:11.600 --> 00:37:18.400
can support animated scaling of bones as well.&nbsp;
The transformations will be provided at regular&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:18.400 --> 00:37:24.880
intervals according to some frame rate. For&nbsp;
example, a 10 second animation and 24 frames per&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:24.880 --> 00:37:32.800
second will include 240 sets of transformations,&nbsp;
these sets are usually very close. So if the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:32.800 --> 00:37:38.480
actual frame rate in the game is higher than&nbsp;
the animation frame rate, we can interpolate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:38.480 --> 00:37:44.720
between consecutive transformations and get the&nbsp;
final animation. Since these transformations&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:44.720 --> 00:37:50.240
represent the changes in the orientation of the&nbsp;
bones, we can apply them on the vertices that are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:50.240 --> 00:37:56.640
influenced by these bones and basically animate&nbsp;
the model. Okay, this is enough theory for now.&nbsp;&nbsp;

